  "text": "ANSWER: Microsoft ended standard support for Windows 10 Home and Pro on October 14, 2025. To receive subsequent security updates, Microsoft offered consumers the option to enroll in the Extended Security Updates (ESU) program via a one-time purchase, Microsoft Rewards points, or by syncing PC settings.   \nMicrosoft\n\nCLAIMS:\nC1: Microsoft ended standard support for Windows 10 Home and Pro on October 14, 2025. [1.1.2]\nC2: Microsoft offered consumers the option to enroll in the Extended Security Updates (ESU) program for ongoing security updates. [1.2.3]\nC3: Consumers could obtain ESU coverage through a one-time purchase, redeeming Microsoft Rewards points, or syncing PC settings. [1.2.2]\n\nSOURCES:\n[1.1.2] Windows 10 Home and Pro - Microsoft Lifecycle – Microsoft – not stated – https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/lifecycle/products/windows-10-home-and-pro\n[1.2.2] Windows 10 Consumer Extended Security Updates (ESU) – Microsoft – not stated – https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/extended-security-updates\n[1.2.3] Windows 10 support has ended on October 14, 2025 – Microsoft Support – October 14, 2025 – https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/deployment/updates-lifecycle/windows-10-support-has-ended-on-october-14-2025\n\nCONFIDENCE: high – The facts are directly verified by official Microsoft lifecycle and support documentation."
